  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Temperatures recover to near normal today. \n\nWarm air advection aloft will bring warming temperatures\ntoday. Any morning low level clouds should give way before noon\nwith increasing solar insolation and warm air advection \nresulting in a boost in the temperatures areawide. 850 mb \ntemperatures recover into the +2 to +4 deg C range so look for \nsurface temperatures to top out near the seasonal norms for this\nafternoon. May see some mountain wave mid-high clouds attms, just\nlee of the Blue Ridge where higher level moisture gets enhanced.\nOverall, however, thinking the afternoon should be mostly \nsunny. As for the winds, they are expected to diminish during \nthe afternoon.\n\nConditions will favor a normal nocturnal/diurnal cycle tonight\nand Sunday with Lows/Highs near normal. Sunday may actually turn\nout slightly above normal by 5 degrees depending how quick the\nclouds roll in ahead of our next storm system. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Next chance of rain is Sunday night with risk of\nstrong, gusty winds.\n\nA cold front will approach the area Sunday and pass overhead \nSunday night. Southwest winds will promote warm air advection \nahead front with increasing moisture resulting in increasing \ncloudiness. Model ensembles indicate that 99% of the rain \nassociated with this feature will pass through our CWA between \n00Z-12Z or 7PM Sunday through 7AM Monday. Model forecast rain \namounts have trended up with both the ECMWF and GFS now showing \n0.25 to 0.50 of rainfall. Much of this increase may be due to \nthe fact the convective parameters supporting deep convection \nhave increased. The short wave driving this system is vigorous \nand may allow the convective line of showers that develops over \nthe OH/TN valleys Sunday to be maintained as a quasi-linear \nfeature as it passes over the mountains Sunday night. This would\nfavor higher chance for measurable rainfall in general, in \nother words near 100% chance for 0.01, but also favor higher \nQPF.\n\nWith respect to hazards, there is concern for enhanced wind\ngusts. We are expecting breezy conditions just ahead and behind\nthe front. Models indicate a 60-70kt low level jet at 850 mb\njust ahead of the front. If there is any deep convection, these\nwinds could be mixed to the surface. Attm, do not have any\nheadlines for wind, but confidence is increasing that we may\nneed a wind advisory for parts of the forecast area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Dry weather expected Monday through Thursday, \nwith seasonal temperatures early in the week warming to above \nnormal for the second half.\n\nDry weather returns Monday with near seasonal temperatures\nanticipated for most of next week. Zonal flow aloft is forecast\nfor much of the country early in the week. El Nino will enhance\nanother southern stream storm with impacts to California \nTuesday-Wednesday, the energy from this feature progressing east\nand reaching our part of the world by the weekend. \n\nThe CPC Outlook leans into the southern stream remaining active\nwith the 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks reflecting above normal \nchances for precipitation from coast to coast but skewed to the \nsouthern CONUS. Not a lot of cold air to be found, so most of \nthe precipitation associated with these systems should be rain.\n\n85H temperatures are forecast to warm to around +10 deg C\nThursday-Saturday (Dec 5-7). The NBM has temperatures across \nour area 10-15 degrees above normal for the end of the week.",
